\title{Upstream LLVM libc \code{\verb~|exp2m1f~} triggers undefined behavior on negative NaNs}
\taxon{Problem}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{antigravity}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{semantics}
\tag{difftest}
\tag{ub}
\meta{status}{open}
\meta{created}{2026-11-25}
\meta{updated}{2026-11-25}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} open. This bug lives in upstream LLVM libc at commit \code{\verb~|e1a5f92db023~}. Our Lean model faithfully exposes the undefined behavior as \code{\verb~|Option.none~}.}
\p{In \code{\verb~|src/__support/math/exp2m1f.h~}, the outer branch \code{\verb~|if (xbits.is_pos())~} skips every negative NaN (\code{\verb~|0xFF800001~} through \code{\verb~|0xFFFFFFFF~}). In the negative branch, the guard \code{\verb~|if (x <= -25.0f)~} evaluates to \code{\verb~|false~} on NaN. That makes the inner check \code{\verb~|if (xbits.is_nan()) return x;~} at line 111 unreachable dead code.}
\p{Execution falls through to \code{\verb~|static_cast<int>(x)~} at line 152 (SSE2) or line 149 (SSE4.2 and AVX2+FMA). Converting NaN to \code{\verb~|int~} is undefined behavior in C++. Compiling with \code{\verb~|-fsanitize=float-cast-overflow~} prints a runtime error and produces \code{\verb~|0x00000000~} on x86-64.}
\p{Commit \code{\verb~|a2d2388~} proves \code{\verb~|exp2m1fSse2_ub_at_negNaN~}, \code{\verb~|exp2m1fSse42_ub_at_negNaN~}, and \code{\verb~|exp2m1fAvx2Fma_ub_at_negNaN~} in \code{\verb~|LibcMath/Kernels/Exp2m1f.lean~} with zero \code{\verb~|sorry~}. Differential testing confirms zero mismatches on all 2 174 defined inputs across all four rounding modes. See session [ses-BUYJ](ses-BUYJ).}
